1. Mathematical Preliminaries

1.1 Notation.
Let \mathbb{N}, \mathbb{Z}, \mathbb{R} denote the natural, integer, and real numbers. \mathbb{F}_2 is the field of two elements. For integer N, \log N denotes base‑2 logarithm. \mathbb{P} is the set of primes. For real a,b, \pi(a) counts primes \le a. The factor base \mathcal{F}_B = \{p \in \mathbb{P} : p \le B\} has cardinality \pi(B) \sim B/\log B.

1.2 Integer Factorisation.
Given composite N = pq, we seek non‑trivial divisors. The Quadratic Sieve (QS) exploits the identity x^2 \equiv y^2 \pmod N with x \not\equiv \pm y \pmod N, whence \gcd(x-y,N) yields a factor. QS collects relations u^2 \equiv \prod_{p \in \mathcal{F}_B} p^{e_p} \pmod N; exponent vectors modulo 2 form a sparse matrix over \mathbb{F}_2, and a linear dependency produces the required congruence.

1.3 Smoothness. Definitions and Dickman Asymptotics.
An integer m is B-smooth if all its prime factors are at most B. For a random integer near N, the smoothness probability equals the Dickman–de Bruijn function \rho(u) with u = \log N / \log B. The function satisfies

\rho(u)=1 \quad (0 \le u \le 1), \qquad u\rho'(u)=-\rho(u-1) \quad (u>1),

and for u > 1 the asymptotics yield \rho(u) = u^{-u+o(u)}. For N \sim 2^{100} and B = 10^6, we have u \approx 5.0 and \rho(5) \approx 3.2 \times 10^{-4}.

1.4 Smoothness Oracle as a Boolean Function.
Define, for each candidate r in a sieving interval I and each prime p \in \mathcal{F}_B,

T_p(r) =
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if } p^{\alpha} \mid r^2 \bmod N, \\
0, & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\qquad
\alpha = \lfloor \log_p R \rfloor.

The smoothness of r corresponds to the event that every prime dividing r^2 \bmod N belongs to \mathcal{F}_B. Approximating this condition with a Bloom filter: choose hash functions h_i : \mathcal{F}_B \to \{1,\dots,w\} (i=1,\dots,d) and define bucket ORs

B_{i,b}(r) = \bigvee_{p :\, h_i(p)=b} T_p(r),

with the filter output

Q(r) = \bigvee_{i=1}^d \bigwedge_{b=1}^w B_{i,b}(r).

This is a depth‑3 Boolean formula (OR of ANDs of ORs) with unbounded fan‑in, depending on the vector T(r) \in \{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{F}_B|}.

