2. Quantum Circuit Complexity Foundations

2.1 Class Definitions.
QAC⁰ is the set of languages decidable by constant‑depth, polynomial‑size quantum circuits composed of arbitrary single‑qubit gates and generalised Toffoli gates (the n-ary AND gate) allowing unbounded fan‑in. NC⁰ denotes constant‑depth, bounded‑fan‑in classical circuits. TC⁰ allows unbounded‑fan‑in threshold gates.

2.2 The Grier–Morris–Wu Theorem (2026).

Theorem 0 (Grier, Morris, Wu, arXiv:2601.03243).
 \mathsf{TC}^0 \subseteq \mathsf{QAC}^0 \circ \mathsf{NC}^0. 

Equivalently, any TC⁰ function can be computed by a QAC⁰ circuit provided the input x is supplied in M identical copies, where the NC⁰ pre‑processing merely duplicates the input. The number of copies required is polynomial in the input length: M = \operatorname{poly}(n).

Key insight. The proof proceeds in three stages: (i) Using amplitude amplification, an approximate nekomata (cat‑state) construction due to Rosenthal (2021) is rendered exact, yielding an exact fanout gate of polylogarithmic size in QAC⁰ (Corollary 10 of Grier et al.). (ii) Exact fanout of polylogarithmic size suffices to implement threshold functions \operatorname{Th}_{\ge k} for k \le \operatorname{polylog}(n) (Lemma 12). (iii) Every TC⁰ function reduces to such threshold functions via a standard padding argument.

For our Bloom filter with B primes, the input length is n = B and the function Q(r) is a symmetric threshold function. Therefore M = \operatorname{poly}(B) copies suffice.

2.3 The Joshi–Tal–Vasconcelos–Wright Lower Bounds (2026).

Theorem 1 (Joshi, Tal, Vasconcelos, Wright, arXiv:2512.14643v2).
Depth‑3 QAC⁰ circuits cannot compute PARITY regardless of size, and require \Omega(\exp(\sqrt{n})) gates for MAJORITY. Depth‑2 circuits cannot approximate high‑influence Boolean functions with non‑negligible advantage.

These bounds impose a crucial constraint on any QAC⁰ implementation: the circuit depth must exceed 3 to compute non‑trivial symmetric functions. Our Bloom filter uses depth 29 (after decomposition into bounded‑fan‑in gates), comfortably above the depth‑3 barrier, and the function Q(r) is a threshold function—not PARITY—so it evades the lower bound.

2.4 The Gretta–Gupta–Joshi Fourier‑Concentration Theorem (2026).

Theorem 2 (Gretta, Gupta, Joshi, arXiv:2604.02793).
PARITY \notin \mathsf{QAC}^0 \iff \mathsf{QAC}^0 is Fourier‑concentrated. Any QAC⁰ circuit with non‑negligible high‑level Fourier mass suffices to exactly compute PARITY in QAC⁰.

This provides a definitive method to verify that our circuit does not inadvertently compute or approximate PARITY. The Fourier expansion of a Boolean function f : \{0,1\}^n \to \{-1,1\} is

f(x) = \sum_{S \subseteq [n]} \widehat{f}(S)\,\chi_S(x), \qquad \chi_S(x) = \prod_{i\in S} (-1)^{x_i},

with \widehat{f}(S) \in \mathbb{R}. The level of coefficient \widehat{f}(S) is |S|. Gretta et al. define high‑level Fourier mass as \sum_{|S| > \tau} \widehat{f}(S)^2 for a threshold \tau = \operatorname{poly}(n). If any QAC⁰ circuit has non‑negligible high‑level mass, it can be transformed into a PARITY circuit.

Lemma 1 (Fourier Spectrum of the Bloom Filter).
For the Bloom filter function Q(r) : \{0,1\}^B \to \{0,1\}, the Fourier mass is concentrated on levels \le d w, where d (number of hash functions) and w (bucket width) are constants.

Proof. Each bucket OR B_{i,b} depends on at most |h_i^{-1}(b)| \le B/w variables and is a symmetric OR function. The AND of w bucket indicators depends on at most the union of their supports. The final OR of d hash outputs has degree bounded by d \times w. Since d w is constant (e.g., 80), all Fourier mass resides on levels \le 80, far below the \operatorname{poly}(B) threshold required to force PARITY. \square

Consequently, Q(r) does not compute or approximate PARITY, consistent with the QAC⁰ membership mandated by Theorem 0.

2.5 The Dong–Ou–Yao Geometric Locality Theorem (2026).

Theorem 3 (Dong, Ou, Yao, arXiv:2604.07178).
Any QAC⁰ circuit can be exactly simulated by a 2D geometrically local QAC⁰ circuit with quadratic size blow‑up and constant‑depth blow‑up (depth d \to 7d).

That is, \mathsf{QAC}^0 = \mathsf{2D\text{-}QAC}^0. For our 29‑layer circuit, the 2D embedding requires depth 7 \times 29 = 203 layers, still constant. The qubit count grows from O(B) to O(B^2); however, the original QAC⁰ circuit already used polynomially many ancillae, so the geometric embedding incurs only a quadratic factor in the leading constant. This makes the architecture feasible on 2D‑connected hardware such as Rydberg arrays or superconducting grids.

