3. Quantifying the Multi‑Copy Overhead: M = \operatorname{poly}(B)

3.1 Derivation from Grier et al.

Section C of Grier et al. addresses “copy complexity”. The main theorem shows that any symmetric function can be computed with O(n^2) input copies. For a general TC⁰ circuit of size S and depth D, the reduction to threshold functions introduces a polynomial overhead: M = O(S^c) for some constant c. The TC⁰ circuit representing the Bloom filter has size proportional to B (one threshold gate per bucket, plus AND/OR layers). Thus M = O(B^c) with c \approx 3, consistent with the O(n^2) bound for symmetric functions since our function is block‑symmetric.

3.2 Polylogarithmic Copy Limitations.

Grier et al. observe that if the copy complexity could be reduced to \operatorname{polylog}(n), then \mathsf{QAC}^0 = \mathsf{QAC}^0_{wf}. Our Bloom filter requires M = \operatorname{poly}(B), which is not polylogarithmic, so the construction remains within the polynomial regime and does not collapse the QAC⁰ hierarchy.

3.3 Impact on Resource Count.

Each copy of the input requires O(\log R) qubits. With M = O(B^3), the copy overhead dominates the qubit count: O(B^3 \log R) qubits for the multi‑copy stage alone. However, by the Grier et al. fanout construction, copies are generated in constant depth using the exact fanout gate, and the ancillae are recycled via uncomputation. The physical qubit count after optimisations (including geometric locality) is O(B^3), which for B = 10^6 yields impractically large numbers. We therefore employ time‑division multiplexing: the circuit is executed sequentially on batches of size b \ll B, reducing the active qubit count to O(b^3) while processing all B primes across B/b rounds.

