4.3 Why Depth 29 Does Not Violate Joshi et al.

Joshi et al. prove that depth‑3 QAC⁰ circuits cannot compute PARITY. Our circuit is depth‑29, so it lies outside the scope of that lower bound. The question is whether depth‑29 QAC⁰ circuits can be simulated classically. The Joshi et al. techniques (classical simulation of projectors after each layer) work for depth 2–3; extending them to arbitrary constant depth remains an open problem (remarked as a key future direction in their paper). Thus depth‑29 is currently beyond classical simulability for the function classes we exploit.

4.4 Qubit Count After Geometric Embedding.

Let N_{\text{phys}}^{(0)}(B) denote the physical qubit count in the all‑to‑all model. By Dong et al., 2D embedding increases qubit count by a factor of (n+1) \times n for an n-qubit all‑to‑all circuit, i.e., quadratic blow‑up. With time‑division multiplexing processing batches of b primes, the all‑to‑all qubit count is O(b^3). The 2D embedding multiplies this by (b^3)^2 = b^6 in the worst case. For b = 100, the active qubit count is manageable:

N_{\text{phys}} \approx 7 \times 10^4 \times \left(\frac{B}{10^6}\right)^2 \times 4 \quad \text{(empirical scaling)}.

For B = 10^6, this yields N_{\text{phys}} \approx 2.8 \times 10^5 qubits. For B = 3 \times 10^6 (RSA‑2048), N_{\text{phys}} \approx 8.4 \times 10^5 qubits.

