5. Detection and False‑Positive Analysis Under Multi‑Copy Error

5.1 Idealised Bloom Filter Probabilities (Recap).

For a smooth number with s distinct prime factors, the detection probability for a single hash function is

P_1 = \left(1 - \left(1 - \frac{1}{w}\right)^s\right)^w \approx (1 - e^{-s/w})^w,

and overall

P_{\text{det}} = 1 - (1 - P_1)^d.

The false‑positive probability is bounded by

P_{\text{fp}} \le d \cdot \left(1 - \left(1 - \frac{1}{w}\right)^{\bar s}\right)^w,

where \bar s is the maximum number of coincidental positive prime tests. With w=4, d=20, s \approx 4, we have P_1 \approx 0.157, P_{\text{det}} \approx 0.98, and P_{\text{fp}} \le 0.078.

5.2 Multi‑Copy Error Propagation.

The Grier et al. construction amplifies the success probability of approximate threshold computations using exact amplitude amplification. This process introduces a residual error that must be folded into P_{\text{det}} and P_{\text{fp}}. Let \varepsilon_{\text{copy}} denote the failure probability of the exact threshold circuit given M copies. By Lemma 12 of Grier et al., \operatorname{Th}_{\ge k} \in \mathsf{EQAC}^0 for k \le \operatorname{polylog}(n), meaning zero error after amplification. Thus \varepsilon_{\text{copy}} = 0 for the threshold detection, and the idealised Bloom filter probabilities remain valid.

However, physical gate noise introduces additional error. With per‑gate infidelity \epsilon_g, the effective detection probability becomes

P_{\text{det}}^{\text{eff}} = P_{\text{det}} \cdot (1 - \epsilon_g)^{G},

where G is the number of gates in the critical path. For our 29‑layer circuit with G \approx 7 \times 10^8, \epsilon_g = 10^{-3} would destroy the signal. Layerwise PEC compensates for this, as analysed in Section 7.

5.3 Fourier‑Based Validation of Filter Fidelity.

Using the Gretta et al. equivalence, we can monitor whether the circuit has drifted towards PARITY‑like behaviour by examining the Fourier spectrum of the output distribution. If the high‑level Fourier mass (levels > dw) exceeds a threshold, the circuit is deemed to have exited the QAC⁰ class, triggering recalibration. This provides a rigorous, non‑heuristic stability check.

