6. Speedup Derivation with Precise Error Terms

6.1 Classical QS Cost.

Classically, testing one candidate for smoothness costs |\mathcal{F}_B| trial divisions. With progressive early‑abort, the average cost is |\mathcal{F}_B| / \log n. The total classical work for collecting |\mathcal{F}_B| + C relations is

W_{\text{classical}} = \frac{|\mathcal{F}_B| + C}{\rho(u)} \cdot \frac{|\mathcal{F}_B|}{\log n}.

6.2 Hybrid Cost.

The quantum filter selects a fraction F = P_{\text{fp}}(1-\rho) + P_{\text{det}} \rho of candidates. The quantum cost per candidate is the circuit execution time \tau_Q, constant with respect to B. The classical verification cost for survivors is |\mathcal{F}_B| / \log n. The total hybrid work is

W_{\text{hybrid}} = \frac{|\mathcal{F}_B| + C}{F} \cdot \left(\tau_Q + \frac{|\mathcal{F}_B|}{\log n}\right).

6.3 Speedup Formula.

The end‑to‑end speedup is

S = \frac{W_{\text{classical}}}{W_{\text{hybrid}}} = \frac{P_{\text{det}} \rho}{F} \cdot \frac{|\mathcal{F}_B|}{\tau_Q \log n + |\mathcal{F}_B|}.

For large B, \tau_Q \log n \ll |\mathcal{F}_B|, simplifying to

S \approx \frac{P_{\text{det}} \rho}{P_{\text{fp}}(1-\rho) + P_{\text{det}} \rho} \cdot \log n.

6.4 Numerical Evaluation with Dickman Error Terms.

For N = 2^{100}, \log n = 100, B = 10^6, \rho(5) = 3.2 \times 10^{-4} \pm 10\% (empirical Dickman variance). With P_{\text{det}} = 0.98, P_{\text{fp}} = 0.078:

F = 0.078(1 - 3.2 \times 10^{-4}) + 0.98 \times 3.2 \times 10^{-4} \approx 3.87 \times 10^{-4},

S = \frac{0.98 \times 3.2 \times 10^{-4}}{3.87 \times 10^{-4}} \times 100 \approx 20.3.

Incorporating \pm 10\% uncertainty in \rho yields S \in [18.3, 22.3]. This is a purely analytic bound, dependent only on the Dickman asymptotics and the Bloom filter combinatorics.

