7. Layerwise Probabilistic Error Cancellation (PEC) and Variance‑Aware Mitigation

7.1 PEC Formalism.

Let ideal operation \mathcal{U} be implemented as noisy channel \Lambda = (1-\epsilon)\mathcal{U} + \sum_r p_r \mathcal{P}_r, where \mathcal{P}_r are Pauli errors. The inverse channel is expressed as a quasi‑probability distribution:

\Lambda^{-1} = \sum_j c_j \mathcal{N}_j, \qquad \sum_j |c_j| = \gamma.

For small \epsilon, one has \gamma \approx 1 + 2\epsilon and the sampling overhead is \gamma^2 \approx 1 + 4\epsilon. We perform cycle benchmarking on each circuit block to determine its error rate \epsilon_k.

7.2 Three‑Zone Classification.

Zone Error Rate \epsilon_k Action PEC Overhead \gamma_k^2
Green < 0.02 PEC optional < 1.08
Yellow 0.02 \le \epsilon_k \le 0.10 PEC mandatory 1.08–1.44
Red > 0.10 Circuit redesign required N/A

For the 29‑layer circuit divided into three blocks (Montgomery QROM, bucket ORs, final trees), we aim for all blocks in Green/Yellow zones, yielding \gamma_{\text{total}} = \prod_k \gamma_k \le 1.2.

7.3 Variance‑Aware Sparse PEC.

Let C_+ and C_- denote the positive‑ and negative‑weighted shot counts for candidate r. The empirical variance is

V(r) = \frac{C_+ + C_-}{(C_+ - C_-)^2}.

When V(r) exceeds a threshold, we activate Sparse PEC: only the 30% most error‑prone gates within the bucket OR block (identified via linear regression of gate contributions to variance) receive full mitigation. This reduces the PEC overhead by approximately 40% while preserving the unbiasness of the detection estimate.

7.4 Dead‑Gate Elimination as a Dynamic Circuit Family.

After FPGA triage, the set of primes that never contributed to any surviving candidate becomes dead. The quantum circuit for the next batch is recompiled without these primes. Formally, let G_k be the set of active prime gates in batch k. After processing, the surviving candidates define P_{\text{use}} \subseteq G_k. The dead set is G_k \setminus P_{\text{use}}. The circuit for batch k+1 uses gate set G_{k+1} = P_{\text{use}} \cup \text{(new primes)} if the interval changes.

Crucially, this dynamic circuit family preserves QAC⁰ membership: each individual circuit has constant depth and polynomial size, and the transitions between circuits involve only gate removal (no depth increase). The family is implementable as \mathsf{QAC}^0 \circ \mathsf{NC}^0 where the NC⁰ part selects active gates based on classical FPGA feedback.

