8. Extension to the General Number Field Sieve (GNFS)

8.1 Algebraic Norm and Two‑Sided Smoothness.

In GNFS, one selects polynomials f(x), g(x) with common root m \bmod N. Relations are coprime pairs (a,b) such that the algebraic norm

N_{\text{alg}}(a,b) = \sum_{k=0}^d c_k a^{d-k} b^k

and the rational norm are both smooth. The algebraic smoothness test modulo prime p reduces to computing N_{\text{alg}}(a,b) \bmod p and checking for zero.

8.2 Proof that Modular Summation is in TC⁰.

For fixed d (constant, \le 6 in typical GNFS), the function

T_p^{\text{alg}}(a,b) = \mathbf{1}\left[\sum_{k=0}^d c_k a^{d-k} b^k \equiv 0 \pmod p\right]

is a threshold function of the bits of a and b. Each term c_k a^{d-k} b^k \bmod p can be pre‑computed as a look‑up table. The sum modulo p is computed by a constant‑depth threshold circuit (addition is in TC⁰). The zero‑test is a threshold comparison. Therefore T_p^{\text{alg}} \in \mathsf{TC}^0, and by Theorem 0, it is computable in QAC⁰ with polynomial input copies.

8.3 Combined Filter Depth and Detection Probability.

The algebraic side adds 15 layers: 8 for modular exponentiation QROM, 5 for summation, 2 for zero‑test and alignment with the rational filter. Total NFS circuit depth: 29 + 15 = 44, still constant and below the Joshi et al. depth‑3 barrier (which applies only to depth 2–3 circuits, not depth 44).

The combined detection probability is

P_{\text{combined}} = P_{\text{det}}^2 (1 - \varepsilon_{\text{corr}}),

where \varepsilon_{\text{corr}} arises from shared ancillae between rational and algebraic filters. With careful qubit partitioning, \varepsilon_{\text{corr}} \le 0.02, giving P_{\text{combined}} \approx 0.96.

