Appendix F
Validation and Benchmarking Methodology

F.1 Quantum Advantage Hypothesis Test

The test for quantum advantage uses a one‑sided Z‑test:

H_0: p = p_{\text{noise}}, \quad H_1: p > p_{\text{noise}},

where p_{\text{noise}} is the detection rate of a faded (classical‑equivalent) circuit, calibrated to P_{\text{fp}}. The test statistic is

Z = \frac{\hat{p} - p_{\text{noise}}}{\sqrt{p_{\text{noise}}(1-p_{\text{noise}})/n}}.

For p_{\text{noise}} = 0.05 and expected \hat{p}=0.20, n=400 shots yield Z \approx 6.8 > 5 (a 5\sigma result) with power exceeding 0.95.

F.2 Fourier‑Based QAC⁰ Membership Test

As a structural validation distinct from statistical significance, we implement the Gretta–Joshi witness:

1. Collect K = \operatorname{poly}(B) independent output samples.
2. Compute the empirical Fourier coefficients \widehat{Q}(S) for small subsets.
3. Estimate the residual mass R.
4. If R \le B^{-\omega(1)}, the circuit remains within QAC⁰; otherwise, the circuit has drifted and must be recalibrated.

