Appendix G
Cryptographic Impact Assessment

G.1 Evolution of Quantum Factoring Resource Estimates

The quantum threat timeline has compressed dramatically in recent years. The canonical Gidney–Ekerå estimate of 2019 required 20 million physical qubits and 8 hours to factor RSA‑2048. By 2025, Gidney refined this to under 1 million noisy qubits, a 20‑fold reduction. The Iceberg Quantum Pinnacle Architecture (2026) further reduced the estimate to fewer than 100,000 physical qubits by introducing quantum LDPC codes, achieving an order‑of‑magnitude improvement over prior surface‑code architectures.

G.2 JVG Algorithm and QNTT

The Jesse–Victor–Gharabaghi (JVG) algorithm, published in 2026, proposes replacing Shor’s Quantum Fourier Transform with a Quantum Number Theoretic Transform (QNTT), offloading modular exponentiation to classical processors. The authors claim a 99% reduction in quantum gate count and a runtime of approximately 11 hours for RSA‑2048 decryption using only ~5 000 qubits. While the JVG algorithm has not yet received peer‑reviewed validation, its publication underscores the rapid evolution of hybrid classical‑quantum factoring approaches and reinforces the urgency of cryptographic migration.

